    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (BUILD SUCCESS). `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and both its tests are green.\n\nHere's what I changed to implement the soft-delete:\n\n**Domain model** \u2014 `model/Owner.java`\n- Added a `deleted` boolean column (`nullable = false`) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`. A newly created owner defaults to `false`.\n\n**Delete behavior** \u2014 `service/ClinicServiceImpl.deleteOwner`\n- Now flags the owner deleted and saves it instead of removing the row. The controller already returned `204 No Content` (2xx) and looks the owner up first (404 if missing), so `GET /api/owners/{id}` keeps returning it afterward with `deleted: true`.\n\n**Create-time checks ignore deleted owners** \u2014 the two checks that scan existing owners:\n- `HouseholdDuplicateValidator` (the normally-blocking duplicate check) now filters out `deleted` owners before matching household id.\n- `PossibleDuplicateOwnerDetector` (the identity check) now filters out `deleted` owners before matching last name/postcode/telephone.\n\nSo a recreate that would normally be blocked is allowed when the only matching owner has been soft-deleted.\n\n**API contract** \u2014 `resources/openapi.yml`\n- Added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` serializes the flag (MapStruct maps it automatically via the entity accessor).\n\n**Schema** \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` column to the `owners` table in all four DB schemas (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) to keep them consistent.\n\nI kept the logic where it belongs: the soft-delete mechanics in the service facade, the \"ignore deleted\" rule inside the two existing validators (rather than globally in `findAllOwners`, which would have silently altered unrelated counters like namesake/city/household-size), and no new catch-all class.",
